1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:03,462
Welcome to the GP-RareLink deep dive.

2
00:00:03,462 --> 00:00:07,775
This is the long version of the project overview: just under an hour,

3
00:00:07,775 --> 00:00:11,088
following the full interim report section by section.

4
00:00:11,088 --> 00:00:21,062
GP-RareLink is run by the Rare Genomics Institute with the Stanford Open Virtual Assistant Lab and funded by Anthropic's AI for Science program.

5
00:00:21,062 --> 00:00:26,515
The project reads published case reports on six rare genetic diseases with a language model,

6
00:00:26,515 --> 00:00:32,619
turns them into structured per-individual records in which every value carries a quote from its source,

7
00:00:32,619 --> 00:00:37,538
and then checks those records against ClinVar and OMIM, which the model never sees.

8
00:00:37,538 --> 00:00:41,238
What you will hear is an interim report for internal review.

9
00:00:41,238 --> 00:00:45,549
The numbers are real and traceable; the conclusions are provisional,

10
00:00:45,549 --> 00:00:49,100
and the report says plainly which questions remain open.

11
00:00:50,402 --> 00:00:52,065
The talk has four parts.

12
00:00:52,065 --> 00:00:59,177
Part one sets out the problem and the question, and the five outcomes we committed to measure before the main run.

13
00:00:59,177 --> 00:01:04,744
Part two walks through the methods: how the corpus was built, how the model reads a paper,

14
00:01:04,744 --> 00:01:11,240
how every value is checked twice, how records are made, and how the answer key is kept behind a firewall.

15
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:17,874
Part three is the results, in the order of the report: the corpus, extraction and the gatekeeper, stability,

16
00:01:17,874 --> 00:01:25,185
the records, the human audit that has not yet happened, concordance with ClinVar, the OMIM holdout, the gap candidates,

17
00:01:25,185 --> 00:01:31,389
the bias arm, the open-weight comparison, the exploratory analyses, one slide per disease, and spend.

18
00:01:31,389 --> 00:01:38,077
Part four is what it all means, the gaps in the study and a costed plan to close them, and what we are asking of you.

19
00:01:38,077 --> 00:01:44,877
If you prefer to read, the dashboard has the eight-page paper and the full report with every table and interval.

20
00:01:46,178 --> 00:01:46,966
Part one.

21
00:01:46,966 --> 00:01:48,928
The problem and the question.

22
00:01:50,234 --> 00:01:52,884
Here is the problem in one example.

23
00:01:52,884 --> 00:02:01,671
For Alexander disease, a 2024 review counts 182 distinct causative variants in 550 published patients.

24
00:02:01,671 --> 00:02:07,280
ClinVar, the public database laboratories query when they interpret a test,

25
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:14,084
holds 76 pathogenic or likely pathogenic records for the gene in the release of 2026-10-12.

26
00:02:14,084 --> 00:02:17,134
The rest were published, once, and never submitted.

27
00:02:17,134 --> 00:02:21,534
A laboratory testing the next child searches ClinVar and finds nothing.

28
00:02:21,534 --> 00:02:29,194
The knowledge exists, but it is scattered across thousands of papers in prose and tables that no database can query,

29
00:02:29,194 --> 00:02:31,572
and hand curation has never kept up.

30
00:02:31,572 --> 00:02:35,728
The project asks whether a language model, reading with strict rules,

31
00:02:35,728 --> 00:02:40,729
can do the first pass of curation at an accuracy we can measure rather than assume,

32
00:02:40,729 --> 00:02:43,922
and whether that closes a meaningful part of the gap.

33
00:02:45,242 --> 00:02:48,405
Three design choices make the answer checkable.

34
00:02:48,405 --> 00:02:54,112
First, quote everything: a value without a verbatim quote from the paper is not allowed,

35
00:02:54,112 --> 00:02:59,819
a script confirms every quote is really in the text, and not reported is a valid answer,

36
00:02:59,819 --> 00:03:02,867
so a paper's silence is never turned into a no.

37
00:03:02,867 --> 00:03:10,621
Second, hold out the answer key: ClinVar and OMIM are never shown to the model, code that reads papers cannot read them,

38
00:03:10,621 --> 00:03:17,342
a test in the repository fails if it could, and the comparison happens afterwards in a separate harness.

39
00:03:17,342 --> 00:03:20,003
Third, decide the rules first: the prompts,

40
00:03:20,003 --> 00:03:26,439
the forms and the analysis plan were frozen on the twentieth of October 2026, after a one-disease pilot,

41
00:03:26,439 --> 00:03:32,380
and every change since is logged as a numbered deviation approved by the principal investigator.

42
00:03:33,698 --> 00:03:36,335
The plan registers five outcomes.

43
00:03:36,335 --> 00:03:43,401
O1, the only confirmatory one, is a human audit of 250 extracted values against their quoted evidence;

44
00:03:43,401 --> 00:03:46,310
it passes if at most four are unsupported.

45
00:03:46,310 --> 00:03:51,585
The sample is drawn and the blinded rating pages are built, but no claim has been rated.

46
00:03:51,585 --> 00:03:57,672
O2 is concordance with ClinVar and OMIM on gene, variant, classification and phenotype,

47
00:03:57,672 --> 00:04:01,660
reported as estimates with intervals and no pass or fail.

48
00:04:01,660 --> 00:04:10,123
The ClinVar half is done for all six diseases and the OMIM half was done on the twenty-eighth of October against frozen entry pages.

49
00:04:10,123 --> 00:04:17,223
O3 is gap closure: 214 candidates are listed with dossiers, none adjudicated.

50
00:04:17,223 --> 00:04:24,445
O4 is ascertainment bias against natural-history cohorts, with a memorization probe; the probe is done,

51
00:04:24,445 --> 00:04:27,461
the frequency comparison waits on a person.

52
00:04:27,461 --> 00:04:34,949
O5 is efficiency and the comparison with an open-weight model; the run and gatekeeper are done on two diseases,

53
00:04:34,949 --> 00:04:36,635
the blinded audit is not.

54
00:04:37,946 --> 00:04:38,784
Part two.

55
00:04:38,784 --> 00:04:39,721
Methods.

56
00:04:41,042 --> 00:04:45,494
The six diseases were chosen to span inheritance modes and organ systems,

57
00:04:45,494 --> 00:04:49,579
and all are absent from the main public collection of phenopackets.

58
00:04:49,579 --> 00:04:58,137
A20 haploinsufficiency is autosomal dominant and auto-inflammatory; SSADH deficiency is recessive and neurometabolic;

59
00:04:58,137 --> 00:05:01,794
Tangier disease is recessive and a lipid disorder;

60
00:05:01,794 --> 00:05:10,425
leukocyte adhesion deficiency type one is recessive and immunological; Alexander disease is dominant, usually de novo,

61
00:05:10,425 --> 00:05:16,130
and a leukodystrophy; Danon disease is X-linked and cardiac and neuromuscular.

62
00:05:16,130 --> 00:05:21,724
The ClinVar counts are distinct variants on single-gene records in one dated release,

63
00:05:21,724 --> 00:05:29,030
and the OMIM counts are the allelic variants on each gene's entry page, frozen on the twenty-eighth of October.

64
00:05:29,030 --> 00:05:34,755
OMIM calls its list selected examples, so it is a sample, not a catalogue.

65
00:05:34,755 --> 00:05:41,556
A20 haploinsufficiency was the development disease: every extraction rule was tuned on it during the pilot,

66
00:05:41,556 --> 00:05:46,705
so its results flatter the pipeline and the other five form the confirmatory set.

67
00:05:48,026 --> 00:05:55,345
The corpus came from PubMed searches from inception to a recorded date, gene symbol plus disease names and synonyms,

68
00:05:55,345 --> 00:05:58,626
with backward citation searching of the key reviews.

69
00:05:58,626 --> 00:06:04,563
No ClinVar or OMIM citation lists were used as seeds for the five new diseases.

70
00:06:04,563 --> 00:06:11,174
Screening ran in two passes: title and abstract screening by model helpers with a logged reason per record,

71
00:06:11,174 --> 00:06:17,228
then full-text screening of every open full text with a verbatim evidence quote checked by script;

72
00:06:17,228 --> 00:06:20,563
a twenty-item human check settled the screening calls.

73
00:06:20,563 --> 00:06:26,934
Eligible papers report individual-level genotype or phenotype for at least one person with the disease;

74
00:06:26,934 --> 00:06:32,501
reviews with a per-patient table are included and their people tagged as secondary-source.

75
00:06:32,501 --> 00:06:39,774
Access was open access only: PubMed Central, Europe PMC and open copies found through Unpaywall,

76
00:06:39,774 --> 00:06:42,501
with no institutional subscriptions.

77
00:06:42,501 --> 00:06:52,876
In all, 3,553 records were screened, 1,496 entered the corpus, and 421 had open full text.

78
00:06:52,876 --> 00:06:57,663
Natural-history papers for the bias arm were held out of the extraction corpus.

79
00:06:58,970 --> 00:07:05,964
Extraction runs as Claude Code helper agents on the funded account: one helper per paper, reading only the paper,

80
00:07:05,964 --> 00:07:09,182
the instruction file, the schema and the rules file,

81
00:07:09,182 --> 00:07:14,257
with no web access and an instruction to use nothing it remembers about the paper.

82
00:07:14,257 --> 00:07:21,978
The frozen files are the extraction instructions version six, a five-table schema covering publications, individuals,

83
00:07:21,978 --> 00:07:29,632
variants, phenotypic features and a phenotype checklist, a rules file, and the gatekeeper instructions version four.

84
00:07:29,632 --> 00:07:36,630
For the five new diseases the prompt and schema were instantiated by script, swapping only the disease name,

85
00:07:36,630 --> 00:07:40,582
the gene symbol, the schema path and a twenty-item checklist.

86
00:07:40,583 --> 00:07:45,578
Every asserted value carries a verbatim quote; not reported carries no quote;

87
00:07:45,578 --> 00:07:52,585
three-state phenotypes are never collapsed; variant fields are taken only from statements about this person;

88
00:07:52,585 --> 00:07:57,062
values stacked in multi-patient cells are never assigned by position;

89
00:07:57,062 --> 00:08:02,383
and a normal finding is recorded as the abnormality it rules out, marked excluded.

90
00:08:02,383 --> 00:08:09,198
Each disease has twenty disease-specific checklist features, each with a three-state status for every individual,

91
00:08:09,198 --> 00:08:13,420
written from a consensus guideline or review with every quote checked.

92
00:08:14,738 --> 00:08:17,101
Every value is checked twice.

93
00:08:17,101 --> 00:08:24,657
The span check is deterministic: it looks up every quoted span in the source text and demands a verbatim match,

94
00:08:24,657 --> 00:08:26,563
on all values, not a sample.

95
00:08:26,563 --> 00:08:30,922
A helper that left quotes empty was resumed for a repair-only pass,

96
00:08:30,922 --> 00:08:35,476
and each repair was diffed to show that only the target cells changed.

97
00:08:35,476 --> 00:08:37,976
The gatekeeper is a second model read.

98
00:08:37,976 --> 00:08:40,268
For each claim that is not bookkeeping,

99
00:08:40,268 --> 00:08:45,088
a helper asks whether the quote entails the value for the person the row is about.

100
00:08:45,088 --> 00:08:52,662
It records whom the quote is actually about, this person, a named group, the cohort, another person, the paper,

101
00:08:52,662 --> 00:08:59,963
or unclear, and gives one of four verdicts: supported, partially supported, unsupported, or span not found.

102
00:08:59,963 --> 00:09:03,826
No value is exempted because its text matches its quote.

103
00:09:03,826 --> 00:09:09,763
Phenopackets keep only claims judged supported, and the count removed is reported.

104
00:09:09,763 --> 00:09:16,826
One sentence from the plan matters more than any other here: the gatekeeper is a filter, not a measure.

105
00:09:16,826 --> 00:09:22,650
In the pilot its own unsupported rate understated the human audit's by an order of magnitude.

106
00:09:23,954 --> 00:09:27,354
The extracted rows become records in four steps.

107
00:09:27,354 --> 00:09:33,927
Deduplication merges only on an explicit citation tying a person to an earlier report, one to one;

108
00:09:33,927 --> 00:09:40,567
the pilot's sex-and-onset route was dropped after it produced all six false merges of ninety-three.

109
00:09:40,567 --> 00:09:46,990
Normalization runs every variant through VariantValidator on the MANE Select transcript for the gene,

110
00:09:46,990 --> 00:09:53,858
keeps the original string beside the normalized form, flags transcripts that were assumed or version-mapped,

111
00:09:53,858 --> 00:09:57,991
and passes only normalization fields, never database annotations.

112
00:09:57,991 --> 00:10:04,497
Binding maps checklist items to HPO terms through per-disease maps built from a pinned HPO release,

113
00:10:04,497 --> 00:10:09,753
binds free-text features first by script and then by helpers with a lookup tool,

114
00:10:09,753 --> 00:10:15,141
and takes disease terms from Mondo by name, never through an OMIM cross-reference.

115
00:10:15,141 --> 00:10:23,923
The output is one GA4GH phenopacket per identity, validated against the schema, with per-disease settings for gene,

116
00:10:23,923 --> 00:10:27,054
transcript, Mondo term and checklist map.

117
00:10:28,370 --> 00:10:34,233
Putting it together: the five blue boxes are the reading side, run by the frozen pipeline.

118
00:10:34,233 --> 00:10:40,527
Papers go to one reader each; every value gets a quote; the script checks every quote;

119
00:10:40,527 --> 00:10:45,870
the gatekeeper re-reads every claim; the surviving claims become records.

120
00:10:45,870 --> 00:10:48,645
The dashed orange line is the firewall.

121
00:10:48,645 --> 00:10:54,697
Below it, in grey, is the answer key the reader never sees: a dated ClinVar release,

122
00:10:54,697 --> 00:11:01,758
the OMIM entry pages frozen on the twenty-eighth of October, and two published variant catalogues.

123
00:11:01,758 --> 00:11:07,745
The comparison harness reads the records and the answer key and lists what the databases are missing.

124
00:11:07,745 --> 00:11:10,837
The orange box on the right is what has not happened:

125
00:11:10,837 --> 00:11:14,920
the blinded human audit and the expert adjudication of the candidates.

126
00:11:14,920 --> 00:11:18,583
That is where you come in, and we will return to it at the end.

127
00:11:19,898 --> 00:11:23,386
Ground-truth isolation is enforced, not promised.

128
00:11:23,386 --> 00:11:31,434
Code under the extraction package never reads reference data, and an automated test fails if any such path, loader,

129
00:11:31,434 --> 00:11:35,423
or even the tokens ClinVar, OMIM or harness appear there.

130
00:11:35,423 --> 00:11:42,048
Reference snapshots live outside the repository, and only their manifests and counts are committed.

131
00:11:42,048 --> 00:11:45,698
The harness reads the extracted records and the snapshots.

132
00:11:45,698 --> 00:11:50,383
For ClinVar that is the variant summary file of the twelfth of October release,

133
00:11:50,383 --> 00:11:54,061
restricted to single-gene records on the current genome build.

134
00:11:54,061 --> 00:11:57,986
For phenotypes it uses the HPO annotation file.

135
00:11:57,986 --> 00:12:01,916
Two published catalogues serve as completeness benchmarks,

136
00:12:01,916 --> 00:12:06,998
Chen 2020 for A20 haploinsufficiency and Grossi 2024 for Alexander disease;

137
00:12:06,998 --> 00:12:12,148
the other four candidate catalogues failed the registered suitability check.

138
00:12:12,148 --> 00:12:19,736
For OMIM, the principal investigator printed the six phenotype and six gene entry pages on the twenty-eighth of October;

139
00:12:19,736 --> 00:12:21,569
a harness script parses them,

140
00:12:21,569 --> 00:12:27,323
and each allelic variant was placed in ClinVar through ClinVar's own OMIM cross-references.

141
00:12:27,323 --> 00:12:33,798
OMIM's clinical synopsis pages were not frozen, so OMIM's phenotype half is not yet assessed.

142
00:12:35,114 --> 00:12:37,427
The statistics follow the registered plan.

143
00:12:37,427 --> 00:12:37,889
For O1,

144
00:12:37,889 --> 00:12:45,679
250 claims were drawn by simple random sampling stratified by disease from the claims the gatekeeper judged supported,

145
00:12:45,679 --> 00:12:49,839
by script with a fixed seed and before any concordance was run.

146
00:12:49,839 --> 00:12:57,148
Two panel members rate blind on pages that never show the gatekeeper's verdict; a third resolves disagreements;

147
00:12:57,148 --> 00:12:59,914
the pass rule is at most four unsupported.

148
00:12:59,914 --> 00:13:08,464
For O2, tier one is the same genomic allele; tier two is the same protein change where a paper gives no cDNA.

149
00:13:08,464 --> 00:13:15,546
HGVS recall is the share of ClinVar's own paper-and-variant citation pairs, restricted to corpus papers,

150
00:13:15,546 --> 00:13:18,202
that the run extracted from that paper.

151
00:13:18,202 --> 00:13:23,764
Overlap is the share of extracted alleles present in ClinVar, which is not precision.

152
00:13:23,764 --> 00:13:29,664
Classification compares the authors' call with ClinVar's aggregate among stated values.

153
00:13:29,664 --> 00:13:35,114
Intervals are Wilson for proportions and exact for the audit and the gatekeeper shares.

154
00:13:35,114 --> 00:13:40,481
For O3, a gap candidate is a tier-one allele absent from the ClinVar snapshot,

155
00:13:40,481 --> 00:13:47,568
with identifying values the gatekeeper judged supported in at least one row, and, since the OMIM check,

156
00:13:47,568 --> 00:13:50,389
not listed among OMIM's allelic variants.

157
00:13:50,389 --> 00:13:58,277
For O4, a person lists each comparator's cohort frequencies before any literature frequency is computed.

158
00:13:58,277 --> 00:14:03,589
For O5, the open-weight model runs the same papers, prompts and scoring.

159
00:14:04,898 --> 00:14:11,006
A one-disease pilot on A20 haploinsufficiency ran from the eighteenth to the twentieth of October:

160
00:14:11,006 --> 00:14:15,868
two full extraction runs, a fifteen-paper repeat, a hand check of every merge,

161
00:14:15,868 --> 00:14:23,161
two fifty-claim human audits by the principal investigator, ClinVar concordance and the Chen 2020 completeness check.

162
00:14:23,161 --> 00:14:29,186
Its findings set the rules frozen as extraction version six and gatekeeper version four.

163
00:14:29,186 --> 00:14:33,467
The disease was then re-extracted from scratch with the frozen pipeline;

164
00:14:33,467 --> 00:14:37,036
the pilot's own records are not used anywhere in the report.

165
00:14:37,036 --> 00:14:41,648
Four dated deviations followed, each approved before it took effect.

166
00:14:41,648 --> 00:14:47,123
D-38 instantiated the frozen prompt and schema per disease.

167
00:14:47,123 --> 00:14:54,211
D-39 transcribed the Alexander disease catalogue on the harness side after its supplement arrived late.

168
00:14:54,211 --> 00:14:59,261
D-40 made the record-building scripts read per-disease settings.

169
00:14:59,261 --> 00:15:06,111
And D-43, found through the OMIM holdout, added isoform-sensitive positions to normalization:

170
00:15:06,111 --> 00:15:11,563
where a gene has alternative coding exons and a paper names no transcript,

171
00:15:11,563 --> 00:15:17,898
the paper's own exon label chooses the transcript, or the variant gets no genomic key.

172
00:15:17,898 --> 00:15:20,036
We will see the case that forced it.

173
00:15:21,338 --> 00:15:22,326
Part three.

174
00:15:22,326 --> 00:15:24,688
Results, in the order of the report.

175
00:15:25,994 --> 00:15:30,132
The corpus table shows the binding constraint of the whole study.

176
00:15:30,132 --> 00:15:40,516
Across the six diseases, 3,553 records were screened and 1,496 entered the corpus, but only 421 had open full text,

177
00:15:40,516 --> 00:15:47,469
from 14 percent for Tangier disease to 53 percent for A20 haploinsufficiency.

178
00:15:47,469 --> 00:15:55,981
Every disease cleared the registered floor of fifteen eligible primary papers, so no substitute disease was needed,

179
00:15:55,981 --> 00:15:58,719
and 350 papers were extracted in all.

180
00:15:58,719 --> 00:16:01,719
But paywalled papers are not a random subset.

181
00:16:01,719 --> 00:16:07,956
For A20 haploinsufficiency they include the two largest sources of the Chen 2020 benchmark,

182
00:16:07,956 --> 00:16:13,782
and for the other diseases the catalogue reviews' own source papers are often closed.

183
00:16:13,782 --> 00:16:20,307
Keep this in mind for every completeness and recall figure that follows: they are measured on what could be read.

184
00:16:21,626 --> 00:16:23,651
Now the extraction itself.

185
00:16:23,651 --> 00:16:36,301
350 papers produced 2,948 individual rows and 2,233 variant rows, and 241,730 values with a quote.

186
00:16:36,301 --> 00:16:43,067
The span check found every one of those quotes verbatim in its paper: zero problems in every disease,

187
00:16:43,067 --> 00:16:47,489
so the fabricated-span count is zero by construction and by check.

188
00:16:47,489 --> 00:16:53,001
The gatekeeper judged 80,948 model claims.

189
00:16:53,001 --> 00:17:02,560
It found 294 unsupported, which is 0.36 percent; on the five confirmatory diseases, 0.43 percent,

190
00:17:02,560 --> 00:17:06,601
with an exact interval from 0.38 to 0.49.

191
00:17:06,601 --> 00:17:11,839
Per disease the share runs from 0.20 to 0.61 percent.

192
00:17:11,839 --> 00:17:17,201
Adding partially supported verdicts gives about three percent not fully supported.

193
00:17:17,201 --> 00:17:18,840
And the failures cluster:

194
00:17:18,840 --> 00:17:25,526
in five of six diseases the three worst papers hold half or more of that disease's unsupported claims.

195
00:17:25,526 --> 00:17:28,988
This is the filter's reading, and it is not an error rate.

196
00:17:30,290 --> 00:17:31,615
Why do claims fail?

197
00:17:31,615 --> 00:17:36,806
The commonest unsupported field in five diseases is the gene symbol on a variant row:

198
00:17:36,806 --> 00:17:43,402
a relative's or a cited patient's variant written into the proband's row from a sentence that mentions both.

199
00:17:43,402 --> 00:17:48,158
Extraction version five's rule, variant fields only from this person,

200
00:17:48,158 --> 00:17:53,740
holds for probands and slips for relatives the paper says carry the same variant.

201
00:17:53,740 --> 00:17:59,897
The second pattern is hedged wording, suggested, suspected, consistent with, typically,

202
00:17:59,897 --> 00:18:08,390
recorded as observed or excluded; extraction version six has no hedge rule, a gap known and left in place at the freeze.

203
00:18:08,390 --> 00:18:12,140
The third is cohort-level statements applied to individuals;

204
00:18:12,140 --> 00:18:17,515
the cohort in general dominates the unsupported claims in Danon and Alexander disease.

205
00:18:17,515 --> 00:18:22,024
Danon's three worst papers hold 60 of its 110 unsupported claims:

206
00:18:22,024 --> 00:18:28,821
variant and diagnosis fields given to eight family carriers when the paper names only the proband,

207
00:18:28,821 --> 00:18:35,203
usually mild applied to individuals, and exclusions resting on typically showed no symptoms.

208
00:18:35,203 --> 00:18:42,700
The gatekeeper also flagged about thirty papers whose table and text disagree; those claims were judged on the table,

209
00:18:42,700 --> 00:18:45,328
noted, and collected for the human audit.

210
00:18:46,634 --> 00:18:48,134
How stable is the reading?

211
00:18:48,134 --> 00:18:52,066
A random ten percent of each disease's papers, 38 in all,

212
00:18:52,066 --> 00:18:57,584
was re-extracted with the same frozen files and compared with the run of record.

213
00:18:57,584 --> 00:19:02,889
Checklist statuses agree on 97 to 100 percent of cells in every disease:

214
00:19:02,889 --> 00:19:08,709
fixing the feature names removes the wording variance that free-text rows show.

215
00:19:08,709 --> 00:19:14,169
Variant keys agree at 83 to 100 percent; the two lowest, Tangier and Danon,

216
00:19:14,169 --> 00:19:20,284
are each one paper where both runs recorded the same variants in different notation.

217
00:19:20,284 --> 00:19:28,731
Individual counts differ in 7 of 38 papers, from scope calls on relatives and on patients cited from earlier reports,

218
00:19:28,731 --> 00:19:32,197
the same issue the pilot found in cohort papers.

219
00:19:32,197 --> 00:19:35,847
That is the measure to watch when the records are used for counting people.

220
00:19:37,154 --> 00:19:38,479
The records table.

221
00:19:38,479 --> 00:19:49,550
2,948 rows became 2,836 identities after 112 merges, and 2,836 phenopackets, all schema-valid,

222
00:19:49,550 --> 00:19:55,204
1,492 of them carrying a genomic interpretation.

223
00:19:55,204 --> 00:20:02,029
SSADH deficiency's 58 merges are all the duplicate study; Tangier and LAD-I have no citation merges at all,

224
00:20:02,029 --> 00:20:07,579
which is more likely a limit of the citation route than a fact about those literatures.

225
00:20:07,579 --> 00:20:13,847
Normalization of cDNA descriptions ranges from 79 percent in Alexander disease,

226
00:20:13,847 --> 00:20:20,591
where papers print cDNA without the c prefix and use legacy numbering, to 97 percent.

227
00:20:20,591 --> 00:20:24,591
Protein-only strings can match ClinVar only at tier two.

228
00:20:24,591 --> 00:20:29,542
HPO binding covers 84 to 93 percent of feature rows.

229
00:20:29,542 --> 00:20:35,517
The records hold 12,679 observed and 3,371 excluded terms; conflicts,

230
00:20:35,517 --> 00:20:42,012
the same term observed and excluded for one identity from different papers,

231
00:20:42,012 --> 00:20:46,341
are few and are kept visible rather than resolved.

232
00:20:46,341 --> 00:20:51,442
The gatekeeper removed 1,060 claims before the records were built.

233
00:20:52,754 --> 00:20:58,917
A sanity check on the records: the variant spectrum per disease matches what the biology predicts.

234
00:20:58,917 --> 00:21:05,494
Missense variants dominate Alexander disease, a gain-of-function leukodystrophy, and SSADH deficiency,

235
00:21:05,494 --> 00:21:11,233
while nonsense and frameshift variants dominate A20 haploinsufficiency and Danon disease,

236
00:21:11,233 --> 00:21:13,554
which are loss-of-function diseases.

237
00:21:13,554 --> 00:21:19,018
The most frequently observed features per disease are also the expected ones:

238
00:21:19,018 --> 00:21:26,966
oral ulcers and recurrent fever in A20 haploinsufficiency, developmental delay and seizures in SSADH deficiency,

239
00:21:26,966 --> 00:21:32,430
low HDL cholesterol in Tangier disease, leukocytosis and omphalitis in LAD-I,

240
00:21:32,430 --> 00:21:39,029
white matter abnormality in Alexander disease, left ventricular hypertrophy in Danon disease.

241
00:21:39,029 --> 00:21:45,828
And the records carry not reported far more often than a value, as the three-state design requires:

242
00:21:45,828 --> 00:21:51,529
onset age is asserted for a minority of individuals and consanguinity for very few.

243
00:21:51,529 --> 00:21:57,616
Sex is unknown for a sizeable share of identities, mostly people from review tables.

244
00:21:58,922 --> 00:22:01,859
Now the most important slide in the results.

245
00:22:01,859 --> 00:22:07,497
The primary outcome, the human audit of 250 claims, has not been measured.

246
00:22:07,497 --> 00:22:13,284
The sample is drawn, the two blinded rating pages are built, and no claim has been rated.

247
00:22:13,284 --> 00:22:17,434
What we do have are the pilot's two audits, before the pipeline was frozen.

248
00:22:17,434 --> 00:22:22,872
The first, fifty claims from the second pilot run, found 3 unsupported, 6.0 percent,

249
00:22:22,872 --> 00:22:27,209
all variant fields taken from a group statement or a summary table.

250
00:22:27,209 --> 00:22:30,159
The gatekeeper of the day flagged none of them.

251
00:22:30,159 --> 00:22:38,165
The second, after fixes, 48 claims rated from a twenty-paper re-run, found 3 unsupported again, 6.3 percent,

252
00:22:38,165 --> 00:22:44,909
all ages read by position from stacked multi-patient cells that the rater could not verify.

253
00:22:44,909 --> 00:22:50,972
Each audit exposed an error class, and each class now has a rule in the frozen pipeline.

254
00:22:50,972 --> 00:22:53,997
But nothing has been re-audited by a person since.

255
00:22:53,997 --> 00:22:58,260
So the machine's 0.43 percent sits far below the humans' six,

256
00:22:58,260 --> 00:23:04,059
and until the audit is rated no accuracy claim can be made for the frozen pipeline.

257
00:23:05,378 --> 00:23:07,003
Concordance with ClinVar.

258
00:23:07,003 --> 00:23:09,865
Gene agreement is near ceiling by design.

259
00:23:09,865 --> 00:23:16,665
HGVS recall, the share of ClinVar's own citation pairs in our papers that the run extracted, is low:

260
00:23:16,665 --> 00:23:23,465
at tier one from 10 to 35 percent, and from 15 to 67 percent when protein-level matches are allowed.

261
00:23:23,465 --> 00:23:28,528
Overlap, the share of our placed variants that ClinVar already holds,

262
00:23:28,528 --> 00:23:34,765
runs from 35 percent in LAD-I to 87 percent in SSADH deficiency, a well-covered gene.

263
00:23:34,765 --> 00:23:42,056
Where authors state a classification, it agrees with ClinVar's aggregate 59 to 92 percent of the time,

264
00:23:42,056 --> 00:23:49,703
and the disagreements run almost entirely in one direction: authors say pathogenic, ClinVar says uncertain.

265
00:23:49,703 --> 00:23:56,278
HPO annotation recall, whether each annotated feature was seen in at least one person, is high.

266
00:23:56,278 --> 00:24:01,553
The denominators matter: Tangier's recall rests on six citation pairs.

267
00:24:02,858 --> 00:24:04,383
How to read those numbers.

268
00:24:04,383 --> 00:24:11,268
HGVS recall is low, but it measures something specific: whether the run extracted, from a given paper,

269
00:24:11,268 --> 00:24:14,170
a variant that ClinVar cites to that paper.

270
00:24:14,170 --> 00:24:17,283
The misses concentrate in a handful of papers.

271
00:24:17,283 --> 00:24:22,315
In SSADH deficiency two papers hold almost all of them: Akaboshi 2003,

272
00:24:22,315 --> 00:24:29,720
whose table was scrambled in PDF conversion so that only a dozen of fifty-four families were extracted,

273
00:24:29,720 --> 00:24:35,470
and Brennenstuhl 2020, which tested variants in vitro and describes no patients.

274
00:24:35,470 --> 00:24:41,815
In LAD-I one review, a mutation update whose people are database accession numbers,

275
00:24:41,815 --> 00:24:44,795
holds thirty-seven of forty-one misses.

276
00:24:44,795 --> 00:24:48,541
These are scope and input problems more than reading errors,

277
00:24:48,541 --> 00:24:54,595
but that is a hypothesis until the registered triage of fifty sampled misses is done by a person.

278
00:24:54,595 --> 00:25:01,645
Overlap is not precision: a variant absent from ClinVar may be a real gap or a notation difference.

279
00:25:01,645 --> 00:25:09,162
Classification disagreement runs in one direction, consistent with an expectation stated before registration:

280
00:25:09,162 --> 00:25:14,058
authors call variants pathogenic that ClinVar still lists as uncertain.

281
00:25:15,362 --> 00:25:19,487
Two expert catalogues give the strongest completeness check we have.

282
00:25:19,487 --> 00:25:23,015
For A20 haploinsufficiency, Chen 2020's table:

283
00:25:23,015 --> 00:25:30,224
100% (9 of 9) of the catalogue's variants whose source paper was in the corpus were recovered.

284
00:25:30,224 --> 00:25:40,024
For Alexander disease, Grossi 2024's supplementary table: 96% (54 of 56), and the two misses are notation.

285
00:25:40,024 --> 00:25:43,762
Where the pipeline read the source paper, it found the variant.

286
00:25:43,762 --> 00:25:49,601
The unrestricted figures are lower only because most of the catalogues' source papers are paywalled,

287
00:25:49,601 --> 00:25:51,762
which is the access constraint again.

288
00:25:51,762 --> 00:25:55,012
The other four diseases have no registered catalogue:

289
00:25:55,012 --> 00:26:00,224
their candidate catalogues had no per-variant table naming sources, or were not open.

290
00:26:00,224 --> 00:26:07,348
Extending catalogue-based completeness to those diseases is one of the cheapest ways to strengthen the study,

291
00:26:07,348 --> 00:26:09,962
and it needs no model spend to speak of.

292
00:26:11,282 --> 00:26:14,918
OMIM entered the study on the twenty-eighth of October,

293
00:26:14,918 --> 00:26:21,398
when the principal investigator printed the six phenotype entries and the six gene entries to PDF,

294
00:26:21,398 --> 00:26:27,282
because omim.org refuses automated reads from our environment and no API key is in place.

295
00:26:27,282 --> 00:26:35,162
A harness script parses each page: titles, cross-references, the allelic variants with OMIM's legacy labels,

296
00:26:35,162 --> 00:26:41,582
their dbSNP and ClinVar accessions, the papers each narrative cites, and the edit dates.

297
00:26:41,582 --> 00:26:47,782
The content stays outside the repository because OMIM's terms restrict redistribution.

298
00:26:47,782 --> 00:26:54,569
The gene entries list 82 allelic variants, 75 of them titled for our six diseases.

299
00:26:54,569 --> 00:27:00,857
OMIM calls these selected examples: a curated sample of the literature, not a catalogue.

300
00:27:00,857 --> 00:27:07,819
So recall of OMIM's variants is the meaningful direction, and precision against OMIM is not.

301
00:27:07,819 --> 00:27:09,507
Matching went through ClinVar.

302
00:27:09,507 --> 00:27:17,891
Each allelic variant was placed in ClinVar through ClinVar's own OMIM cross-reference or the accessions OMIM prints;

303
00:27:17,891 --> 00:27:20,782
ClinVar's title then gives current HGVS.

304
00:27:20,782 --> 00:27:26,476
That was necessary because OMIM's own labels use legacy protein numbering:

305
00:27:26,476 --> 00:27:31,169
ABCA1 labels sit sixty residues below the current transcript,

306
00:27:31,169 --> 00:27:36,632
and a LAMP2 label reads leucine 113 where the current numbering is 147.

307
00:27:37,946 --> 00:27:46,220
The result: the run recovered 57 percent, 43 of 75 of the variants OMIM lists for these diseases,

308
00:27:46,220 --> 00:27:50,058
with a Wilson interval from 46 to 68 percent.

309
00:27:50,058 --> 00:27:56,987
By disease it runs from 89 percent, 8 of 9 for A20 haploinsufficiency and 86 percent,

310
00:27:56,987 --> 00:28:02,449
12 of 14 for LAD-I down to 11 percent, 2 of 19 for Tangier disease,

311
00:28:02,449 --> 00:28:11,333
whose allelic variants come from the gene-discovery papers of 1999 to 2003, all in the corpus and all closed.

312
00:28:11,333 --> 00:28:18,865
The column that explains the pattern is on the right: of the 84 papers OMIM cites as sources for these variants,

313
00:28:18,865 --> 00:28:20,008
5 were extracted.

314
00:28:20,008 --> 00:28:25,800
Where the source paper was in the run, the variant was recovered from it 8 times out of 14;

315
00:28:25,800 --> 00:28:27,646
the misses have named causes.

316
00:28:28,946 --> 00:28:29,996
Four lessons.

317
00:28:29,996 --> 00:28:38,240
First, coverage, not reading, is the binding constraint: pooled over every paper OMIM cites for these diseases,

318
00:28:38,240 --> 00:28:46,483
96 sit in our screened corpus with no open copy, 160 were never returned by our searches, and 9 were extracted.

319
00:28:46,483 --> 00:28:53,874
The never-returned papers are mostly pre-1990 descriptions under earlier names for the disease:

320
00:28:53,874 --> 00:29:01,887
LAD-I as Mo1 or LFA-1 deficiency, Danon disease as lysosomal glycogen storage with normal acid maltase,

321
00:29:01,887 --> 00:29:06,321
Tangier disease as familial alpha-lipoprotein deficiency.

322
00:29:06,321 --> 00:29:09,708
A search-term gap for diseases described before their gene.

323
00:29:09,708 --> 00:29:15,485
Second, where the source paper was read, the variant was usually there: 8 of 14,

324
00:29:15,485 --> 00:29:22,200
and the misses are a table scrambled in PDF conversion and two variants stated only in prose,

325
00:29:22,200 --> 00:29:28,771
a glycine to an arginine at amino acid 169, which the record keeps verbatim but cannot key.

326
00:29:28,771 --> 00:29:33,727
Third, at the patient level, for the 9 papers both OMIM and the run cover,

327
00:29:33,727 --> 00:29:40,693
our records agreed with OMIM's curated statements 12 times, were partial 7 times, and contradicted none;

328
00:29:40,693 --> 00:29:45,046
where they differ, the run usually has less, not something wrong.

329
00:29:45,046 --> 00:29:52,623
Fourth, OMIM is fallible too: two allelic variants link to ClinVar records that do not match their own text,

330
00:29:52,623 --> 00:29:58,096
and ten labels differ from ClinVar's protein change, nine by legacy numbering.

331
00:29:58,096 --> 00:30:03,033
A holdout curated by people needs the same provenance checks as our output.

332
00:30:04,346 --> 00:30:09,133
The OMIM check also caught an error of ours, and the story is worth a minute.

333
00:30:09,133 --> 00:30:16,336
Every one of the 215 gap candidates was compared with its gene's allelic variants by allele, cDNA,

334
00:30:16,336 --> 00:30:18,321
protein and notation token.

335
00:30:18,321 --> 00:30:22,678
One Danon candidate, a two-base deletion from Sugie 2018,

336
00:30:22,678 --> 00:30:27,646
collided with an OMIM allelic variant by its deletion token only.

337
00:30:27,646 --> 00:30:33,321
The paper's table places the deletion on exon 9b, the LAMP2B isoform.

338
00:30:33,321 --> 00:30:37,527
LAMP2 has alternative terminal exons 9a, 9b and 9c,

339
00:30:37,527 --> 00:30:44,783
and because the paper named no transcript the run had assumed the standard one, exon 9a.

340
00:30:44,783 --> 00:30:50,433
That produced a genomic allele no database holds, because it is not what the paper reported.

341
00:30:50,433 --> 00:30:58,546
On the right transcript, the variant is OMIM 309060.0001 and a ClinVar record.

342
00:30:58,546 --> 00:31:05,821
It was a normalization artefact, not a gap, and it was withdrawn the same day under deviation D-43.

343
00:31:05,821 --> 00:31:11,875
A new module now computes from the RefSeq records where each gene's coding isoforms diverge,

344
00:31:11,875 --> 00:31:19,246
and normalization lets the paper's own words choose the transcript at such positions, or assigns no genomic key.

345
00:31:19,246 --> 00:31:25,908
The Danon run was re-normalized; exactly two of 165 descriptions changed.

346
00:31:25,908 --> 00:31:32,058
Scoring against two independent references found an error that scoring against one could not.

347
00:31:33,362 --> 00:31:34,774
Now the gap itself.

348
00:31:34,774 --> 00:31:41,937
Across the six diseases the papers yielded 466 distinct variants placed on the genome.

349
00:31:41,937 --> 00:31:48,949
245 are in ClinVar, 168 of them classified pathogenic or likely pathogenic.

350
00:31:48,949 --> 00:31:53,587
221 are absent from ClinVar in two successive releases.

351
00:31:53,587 --> 00:32:01,117
After dropping the alleles whose every supporting row failed the gatekeeper, checking each against OMIM,

352
00:32:01,117 --> 00:32:05,462
and withdrawing the isoform artefact, 214 candidates remain.

353
00:32:05,462 --> 00:32:12,528
Each has an evidence dossier written by helpers and checked by script: every paper reporting it, every quote,

354
00:32:12,528 --> 00:32:18,362
found verbatim, the individuals, the authors' own classification, and flags for the panel.

355
00:32:18,362 --> 00:32:26,216
Separately, 53 variants that papers classify as pathogenic sit in ClinVar only as uncertain or conflicting,

356
00:32:26,216 --> 00:32:32,674
15 of them with evidence the harness judged strong enough to propose a reclassification.

357
00:32:32,674 --> 00:32:34,299
All of these are candidates.

358
00:32:34,299 --> 00:32:39,199
Nothing is submitted without adjudication and the principal investigator's sign-off.

359
00:32:40,514 --> 00:32:50,307
By disease, the candidate list is dominated by LAD-I with 82 and A20 haploinsufficiency with 64; SSADH deficiency,

360
00:32:50,307 --> 00:32:54,601
a well-covered gene, contributes 10 and Tangier 7.

361
00:32:54,601 --> 00:33:00,917
The LAD-I list needs care: 66 of its 82 candidates come only from papers ClinVar itself cites,

362
00:33:00,917 --> 00:33:08,576
most of them from one mutation update whose people are database accession numbers, and the protein check flags 10.

363
00:33:08,576 --> 00:33:12,802
Many may be numbering or normalization slips rather than gaps.

364
00:33:12,802 --> 00:33:19,734
Model-written flags on the dossiers mark 152 of the 215 candidates for the panel's attention:

365
00:33:19,734 --> 00:33:27,859
possible same person reported twice, carriers not attached to a case, notation conflicts, protein mismatches,

366
00:33:27,859 --> 00:33:31,214
review-only sources, mosaic or somatic calls.

367
00:33:31,214 --> 00:33:34,339
These are leads for adjudication, not verdicts.

368
00:33:35,642 --> 00:33:41,558
The bar chart shows each disease's genome-placed variants split by their standing in ClinVar:

369
00:33:41,558 --> 00:33:48,236
pathogenic or likely pathogenic in dark blue, another classification in light blue, candidates in orange,

370
00:33:48,236 --> 00:33:50,717
and absent but not a candidate in grey.

371
00:33:50,717 --> 00:33:56,617
A typical candidate is a frameshift or nonsense variant reported in one or two papers,

372
00:33:56,617 --> 00:33:59,292
with the authors calling it pathogenic.

373
00:33:59,292 --> 00:34:07,343
The dossier gives the panel everything in one place: the normalized description, how each paper wrote it, the quotes,

374
00:34:07,343 --> 00:34:13,054
the individuals, zygosity where stated, the authors' classification, and the flags.

375
00:34:13,054 --> 00:34:20,417
The dashboard lists all of them with PubMed links, and the adjudication page, built and private, is ready for the panel.

376
00:34:20,417 --> 00:34:21,654
Two cautions.

377
00:34:21,654 --> 00:34:25,154
The list is a lower bound set by open access.

378
00:34:25,154 --> 00:34:31,852
And it is not yet a list of real gaps: adjudication will sort real gaps from notation differences,

379
00:34:31,852 --> 00:34:33,492
duplicates and carriers.

380
00:34:34,802 --> 00:34:36,927
The bias arm has two parts.

381
00:34:36,927 --> 00:34:44,177
The memorization probe asks whether the model could be extracting facts it remembers from training rather than facts it reads.

382
00:34:44,177 --> 00:34:46,811
180 papers, thirty per disease,

383
00:34:46,811 --> 00:34:56,752
were stratified by publication date against the model's documented training cutoff of June 2026: 10 after, 51 recent,

384
00:34:56,752 --> 00:34:57,602
119 older.

385
00:34:57,602 --> 00:35:04,302
Two black-box probes ran identically on both models: closed-book recall from the PMID, title and year,

386
00:35:04,302 --> 00:35:11,527
and guided completion of a fifty-word opening, scored against the full-text extraction, never against ClinVar.

387
00:35:11,527 --> 00:35:17,427
In the 10 post-cutoff papers neither model produced a specific recall or a correct variant.

388
00:35:17,427 --> 00:35:24,865
Across all strata Claude claimed specific recall for 4 of 180 papers, all older and for phenotypes only;

389
00:35:24,865 --> 00:35:29,514
the few variant hits were recurrent variants labelled as guesses.

390
00:35:29,514 --> 00:35:34,352
The completions with longer matching runs are formulaic consent sentences.

391
00:35:34,352 --> 00:35:38,769
The probe is small and will grow as post-cutoff papers accumulate,

392
00:35:38,769 --> 00:35:45,127
and black-box probes can miss memorization that changes extraction without surfacing as recall.

393
00:35:45,127 --> 00:35:47,590
But it gives no sign of memorization.

394
00:35:48,890 --> 00:35:54,015
The second part of the bias arm, the frequency deviation, has no numbers yet.

395
00:35:54,015 --> 00:35:58,387
The design is simple and deliberately human-first: for each disease,

396
00:35:58,387 --> 00:36:05,073
a person lists the held-out natural-history cohort's HPO-mappable features and their cohort frequencies,

397
00:36:05,073 --> 00:36:08,030
without looking at our literature frequencies;

398
00:36:08,030 --> 00:36:14,202
only then does a script compute observed over observed plus excluded per feature in our records,

399
00:36:14,202 --> 00:36:18,253
with a publication-cluster bootstrap, and report the deviation.

400
00:36:18,253 --> 00:36:27,034
4 open comparators are fetched and a listing sheet exists for each: A20 haploinsufficiency, Alexander disease,

401
00:36:27,034 --> 00:36:29,828
Danon disease and SSADH deficiency.

402
00:36:29,828 --> 00:36:36,434
Three candidate comparators are paywalled and left out, and LAD-I and Tangier disease have no comparator,

403
00:36:36,434 --> 00:36:39,203
so they are out of the bias arm by decision.

404
00:36:39,203 --> 00:36:43,065
The script is written and tested on synthetic input only.

405
00:36:43,065 --> 00:36:46,565
What it needs is one to two hours of a person per sheet.

406
00:36:47,882 --> 00:36:49,457
The efficiency outcome.

407
00:36:49,457 --> 00:36:54,832
Expert-minutes per accepted record cannot be measured until adjudication starts.

408
00:36:54,832 --> 00:37:00,816
The model comparison is done for two diseases: DeepSeek V4.1 Flash, an open-weight model,

409
00:37:00,816 --> 00:37:07,875
ran the same 85 A20 and 40 SSADH papers with the same launch prompt, frozen instruction files and schema,

410
00:37:07,875 --> 00:37:14,598
through an agent loop with file tools; the outputs went through the same span check, reconciliation,

411
00:37:14,598 --> 00:37:19,170
normalization and harness, and the same gatekeeper judged both runs.

412
00:37:19,170 --> 00:37:29,720
The two models found largely the same variants: 261 of 294 in the union for A20, 157 of 166 for SSADH.

413
00:37:29,720 --> 00:37:35,982
The open model cost about one 15th as much per paper and took 7 times as long.

414
00:37:35,982 --> 00:37:37,995
Where it differed was provenance.

415
00:37:37,995 --> 00:37:46,857
Claude left no value without a quote; the open model left 3.6 and 3.0 percent without one, which the rules forbid.

416
00:37:46,857 --> 00:37:56,857
The gatekeeper judged 8.6 and 6.1 percent of its claims unsupported, against 0.20 and 0.38 for Claude.

417
00:37:58,178 --> 00:38:00,841
Three readings and two cautions.

418
00:38:00,841 --> 00:38:06,216
The readings: the method transfers, in that a much cheaper model finds the same variants;

419
00:38:06,216 --> 00:38:13,040
the provenance rules bind differently on different models, in that the open model drops quotes the rules require;

420
00:38:13,040 --> 00:38:17,328
and quote-level provenance is the cheapest place to see the difference.

421
00:38:17,328 --> 00:38:21,324
Most of the gap in unsupported rates is claims with no quote at all,

422
00:38:21,324 --> 00:38:25,791
but claims with a quote were also judged unsupported several times as often.

423
00:38:25,791 --> 00:38:32,129
The cautions: the judge is Claude, which may not be neutral between a Claude and a non-Claude extraction,

424
00:38:32,129 --> 00:38:36,416
and the gatekeeper helpers did not all treat empty quotes the same way.

425
00:38:36,416 --> 00:38:41,400
The registered measure is a blinded human audit of equal samples from each model,

426
00:38:41,400 --> 00:38:45,153
and it waits on the same panel decision as the primary audit.

427
00:38:45,153 --> 00:38:49,784
The scaffolds also differed slightly, as the plan requires us to state:

428
00:38:49,784 --> 00:38:56,436
the open-weight loop had no shell and no repair passes, and three harness fixes landed during its run,

429
00:38:56,436 --> 00:38:57,741
approved and logged.

430
00:38:57,741 --> 00:39:02,503
Two diseases, one model, one provider: not a general finding.

431
00:39:03,818 --> 00:39:07,381
Two exploratory analyses, flagged as such.

432
00:39:07,381 --> 00:39:14,537
First, genotype-phenotype tests with GPSEA, Fisher exact tests with Benjamini-Hochberg correction,

433
00:39:14,537 --> 00:39:16,143
in a separate project.

434
00:39:16,143 --> 00:39:20,410
Variant effects come from each phenopacket's own normalized HGVS,

435
00:39:20,410 --> 00:39:25,137
only individuals with a placed variant and at least one phenotype enter,

436
00:39:25,137 --> 00:39:31,243
and zygosity for untyped calls is an analysis assumption only; the records keep not reported.

437
00:39:31,243 --> 00:39:39,053
In A20 haploinsufficiency, Alexander disease, SSADH deficiency and LAD-I, no term survives correction,

438
00:39:39,053 --> 00:39:42,805
and Tangier has too few phased genotypes to test.

439
00:39:42,805 --> 00:39:48,415
Danon disease reproduces the published sex difference: males, who are hemizygous,

440
00:39:48,415 --> 00:39:55,618
far more often show proximal muscle weakness and left ventricular hypertrophy than heterozygous females.

441
00:39:55,618 --> 00:40:02,456
The papers overlap with the published series, so this is a reproduction check, not an independent replication,

442
00:40:02,456 --> 00:40:09,418
and the truncating-versus-missense arrhythmia signal rests on a handful of missense carriers and is a lead only.

443
00:40:10,730 --> 00:40:15,505
Second, a look at what ClinVar's ABCA1 record actually contains.

444
00:40:15,505 --> 00:40:24,615
Of 2,006 single-gene ABCA1 records in ClinVar, only 16 match variants extracted from the thirty-one Tangier papers,

445
00:40:24,615 --> 00:40:29,130
and of the 85 pathogenic or likely pathogenic records, 7.

446
00:40:29,130 --> 00:40:34,380
Most of the gene's ClinVar records come from testing unrelated to Tangier disease.

447
00:40:34,380 --> 00:40:40,925
Separating the monogenic allelic series from that noise is itself one of the tests the grant proposed,

448
00:40:40,925 --> 00:40:44,967
and it is a lower bound because the corpus is open access only.

449
00:40:44,967 --> 00:40:47,555
Two negative results on deduplication.

450
00:40:47,555 --> 00:40:54,588
The planned LAD-I benchmark against the duplicate cases in Almarza Novoa 2018 is not computable:

451
00:40:54,588 --> 00:41:00,155
the review's open supplement flags only one overlap and names no case pairs.

452
00:41:00,155 --> 00:41:04,922
And the citation-only merge route found no merges at all in Tangier and LAD-I,

453
00:41:04,922 --> 00:41:09,690
which is more likely a limit of the route than a fact about those literatures;

454
00:41:09,690 --> 00:41:16,230
the dossier helpers later proposed nineteen same-person groups across the candidates, which await a person.

455
00:41:17,546 --> 00:41:19,521
A20 haploinsufficiency.

456
00:41:19,521 --> 00:41:28,808
838 records screened, 160 in the corpus, 85 with open full text, 85 papers extracted.

457
00:41:28,808 --> 00:41:38,925
811 rows became 769 identities, 558 with a variant in the gene; the most observed features are Oral ulcer 43 percent;

458
00:41:38,925 --> 00:41:45,496
Recurrent fever 27 percent; Inflammatory abnormality of the skin 22 percent.

459
00:41:45,496 --> 00:41:50,308
The gatekeeper judged 0.20 percent of claims unsupported.

460
00:41:50,308 --> 00:42:01,996
Against ClinVar, HGVS recall at tiers one and two was 27 percent, 21 of 78 and overlap 38 percent, 39 of 103.

461
00:42:01,996 --> 00:42:10,283
64 candidates are absent from both databases, and 89 percent, 8 of 9 of OMIM's listed variants were recovered.

462
00:42:10,283 --> 00:42:20,058
Development disease: every rule was tuned on its pilot runs; 92 of 160 corpus papers were ClinVar-seeded (D-19).

463
00:42:20,058 --> 00:42:27,063
Treat its figures as an upper bound Completeness vs Chen 2020: 100 percent (9 of 9) restricted;

464
00:42:27,063 --> 00:42:30,233
most catalogue source papers are paywalled.

465
00:42:31,538 --> 00:42:32,875
SSADH deficiency.

466
00:42:32,875 --> 00:42:42,288
357 records screened, 171 in the corpus, 59 with open full text, 40 papers extracted.

467
00:42:42,288 --> 00:42:48,172
421 rows became 363 identities, 243 with a variant in the gene;

468
00:42:48,172 --> 00:42:58,538
the most observed features are Global developmental delay 25 percent; Seizure 20 percent; Hypotonia 18 percent.

469
00:42:58,538 --> 00:43:04,101
The gatekeeper judged 0.38 percent of claims unsupported.

470
00:43:04,101 --> 00:43:15,601
Against ClinVar, HGVS recall at tiers one and two was 15 percent, 23 of 158 and overlap 87 percent, 68 of 78.

471
00:43:15,601 --> 00:43:23,950
10 candidates are absent from both databases, and 57 percent, 4 of 7 of OMIM's listed variants were recovered.

472
00:43:23,950 --> 00:43:33,129
Duplicate study: the preprint and journal version are both in the corpus, their patients merged with evidence recorded;

473
00:43:33,129 --> 00:43:39,222
the papers disagree on G176R vs G176E for one variant O2 misses are two papers:

474
00:43:39,222 --> 00:43:47,476
a table scrambled in conversion (about 12 of 54 families extracted) and an in-vitro study with no patients.

475
00:43:48,794 --> 00:43:50,006
Tangier disease.

476
00:43:50,006 --> 00:43:59,181
529 records screened, 284 in the corpus, 39 with open full text, 31 papers extracted.

477
00:43:59,181 --> 00:44:04,385
235 rows became 235 identities, 65 with a variant in the gene;

478
00:44:04,385 --> 00:44:13,785
the most observed features are Decreased circulating HDL-C concentration 21 percent; Angina pectoris 14 percent;

479
00:44:13,785 --> 00:44:17,394
Coronary artery atherosclerosis 13 percent.

480
00:44:17,394 --> 00:44:22,906
The gatekeeper judged 0.31 percent of claims unsupported.

481
00:44:22,906 --> 00:44:32,956
Against ClinVar, HGVS recall at tiers one and two was 50 percent, 3 of 6 and overlap 56 percent, 9 of 16.

482
00:44:32,956 --> 00:44:40,956
7 candidates are absent from both databases, and 11 percent, 2 of 19 of OMIM's listed variants were recovered.

483
00:44:40,956 --> 00:44:44,756
Smallest corpus and lowest open-access share;

484
00:44:44,756 --> 00:44:52,102
many old papers with tables as images Only 65 of 235 identities carry an ABCA1 variant:

485
00:44:52,102 --> 00:44:57,844
140 come from review tables and many older reports give no genotype.

486
00:44:59,112 --> 00:45:01,217
Leukocyte adhesion deficiency I.

487
00:45:01,217 --> 00:45:09,111
874 records screened, 274 in the corpus, 59 with open full text, 42 papers extracted.

488
00:45:09,111 --> 00:45:14,225
508 rows became 508 identities, 328 with a variant in the gene;

489
00:45:14,225 --> 00:45:22,828
the most observed features are Increased total leukocyte count 32 percent; Neonatal omphalitis 12 percent;

490
00:45:22,828 --> 00:45:25,426
Pyoderma gangrenosum 11 percent.

491
00:45:25,426 --> 00:45:29,636
The gatekeeper judged 0.43 percent of claims unsupported.

492
00:45:29,636 --> 00:45:41,214
Against ClinVar, HGVS recall at tiers one and two was 32 percent, 19 of 60 and overlap 35 percent, 45 of 129.

493
00:45:41,214 --> 00:45:51,213
82 candidates are absent from both databases, and 86 percent, 12 of 14 of OMIM's listed variants were recovered.

494
00:45:51,213 --> 00:45:59,776
The gap list is dominated by one review (van de Vijver 2012) whose people are accession numbers;

495
00:45:59,776 --> 00:46:08,161
many candidates may be numbering slips Input problem: one supplement cut at 60,000 characters,

496
00:46:08,161 --> 00:46:12,264
so patients P75 to P127 have no clinical rows.

497
00:46:13,514 --> 00:46:15,014
Alexander disease.

498
00:46:15,014 --> 00:46:24,739
539 records screened, 363 in the corpus, 83 with open full text, 69 papers extracted.

499
00:46:24,739 --> 00:46:30,347
463 rows became 456 identities, 318 with a variant in the gene;

500
00:46:30,347 --> 00:46:39,606
the most observed features are Abnormal cerebral white matter morphology 19 percent; Seizure 16 percent;

501
00:46:39,606 --> 00:46:41,564
Dysarthria 14 percent.

502
00:46:41,564 --> 00:46:47,264
The gatekeeper judged 0.27 percent of claims unsupported.

503
00:46:47,264 --> 00:46:58,951
Against ClinVar, HGVS recall at tiers one and two was 67 percent, 34 of 51 and overlap 73 percent, 49 of 67.

504
00:46:58,951 --> 00:47:07,651
17 candidates are absent from both databases, and 69 percent, 9 of 13 of OMIM's listed variants were recovered.

505
00:47:07,651 --> 00:47:14,626
Lowest normalization (79 percent): cDNA printed without "c.", legacy numbering;

506
00:47:14,626 --> 00:47:24,779
65 protein-only strings match ClinVar only at tier 2 Completeness vs Grossi 2024: 96 percent (54 of 56) restricted;

507
00:47:24,779 --> 00:47:27,251
the two misses are notation.

508
00:47:28,562 --> 00:47:29,762
Danon disease.

509
00:47:29,762 --> 00:47:39,224
416 records screened, 244 in the corpus, 96 with open full text, 83 papers extracted.

510
00:47:39,224 --> 00:47:44,039
510 rows became 505 identities, 281 with a variant in the gene;

511
00:47:44,039 --> 00:47:52,523
the most observed features are Left ventricular hypertrophy 39 percent; Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy 28 percent;

512
00:47:52,523 --> 00:47:55,274
Congestive heart failure 20 percent.

513
00:47:55,274 --> 00:48:00,437
The gatekeeper judged 0.61 percent of claims unsupported.

514
00:48:00,437 --> 00:48:10,599
Against ClinVar, HGVS recall at tiers one and two was 50 percent, 10 of 20 and overlap 48 percent, 35 of 73.

515
00:48:10,599 --> 00:48:19,287
34 candidates are absent from both databases, and 62 percent, 8 of 13 of OMIM's listed variants were recovered.

516
00:48:19,287 --> 00:48:28,709
Highest gatekeeper unsupported share (0.61 percent): three papers hold 60 of 110 Sex difference reproduced (GPSEA):

517
00:48:28,709 --> 00:48:34,362
males more often show myopathy and hypertrophy; a reproduction check.

518
00:48:35,666 --> 00:48:36,466
Spend.

519
00:48:36,466 --> 00:48:46,749
By this session's own usage readings, about 1,861 dollars of the 45,000 dollar award had been used by 2026-10-24,

520
00:48:46,749 --> 00:48:48,478
about four percent.

521
00:48:48,478 --> 00:48:51,591
Extraction and the gatekeeper account for most of it.

522
00:48:51,591 --> 00:48:57,804
The readings are estimates, not billing figures: they sometimes froze for an hour, stages overlapped,

523
00:48:57,804 --> 00:49:02,541
and whether this session runs on the funded account is still being confirmed.

524
00:49:02,541 --> 00:49:04,685
Measured unit costs on this route:

525
00:49:04,685 --> 00:49:11,053
about two dollars per paper for extraction and under half a cent per judged claim for the gatekeeper;

526
00:49:11,053 --> 00:49:16,853
the six-disease run with its ten percent repeat came in well under its 1,200 dollar ceiling.

527
00:49:16,853 --> 00:49:21,453
The open-weight compute was billed outside the award, about 20 dollars.

528
00:49:21,453 --> 00:49:28,541
A separate exploratory pilot in another session cost about 317 dollars and is not covered in this talk.

529
00:49:28,541 --> 00:49:34,028
The large remaining costs of the project are human hours, which the award does not fund.

530
00:49:35,330 --> 00:49:36,292
Part four.

531
00:49:36,292 --> 00:49:39,667
What it means, the gaps in the study, and what we need from you.

532
00:49:40,970 --> 00:49:42,145
What the data say.

533
00:49:42,145 --> 00:49:46,947
The pipeline runs end to end on six diseases at the scale the grant projected,

534
00:49:46,947 --> 00:49:53,595
with provenance that holds on every value and repeats that are stable where the schema fixes the vocabulary.

535
00:49:53,595 --> 00:50:00,350
It produces 2,836 valid phenopackets, a ClinVar comparison with intervals on every figure,

536
00:50:00,350 --> 00:50:03,803
an OMIM check against a human-curated holdout,

537
00:50:03,803 --> 00:50:09,732
and 214 candidate gaps that two successive ClinVar releases confirm are absent.

538
00:50:09,732 --> 00:50:15,507
What the data do not say: nothing here establishes the accuracy of the frozen pipeline.

539
00:50:15,507 --> 00:50:21,230
The gatekeeper's figure is a filter's reading; the only human-rated figures come from the pilot,

540
00:50:21,230 --> 00:50:24,270
before the rules those audits produced were frozen.

541
00:50:24,270 --> 00:50:28,501
Against OMIM the run recovered 57 percent of the listed variants,

542
00:50:28,501 --> 00:50:32,472
but the misses are mostly papers the pipeline could not read,

543
00:50:32,472 --> 00:50:36,507
so that figure measures corpus coverage as much as extraction.

544
00:50:36,507 --> 00:50:44,219
The gatekeeper's value is in the records, not the metric: it removed 1,060 claims before the phenopackets were built,

545
00:50:44,219 --> 00:50:48,832
and its reasons name the failure modes a person should look for first.

546
00:50:50,138 --> 00:50:55,025
The OMIM holdout gave three things the ClinVar scoring could not.

547
00:50:55,025 --> 00:50:58,400
A direction: where OMIM's source paper was in the run,

548
00:50:58,400 --> 00:51:04,150
the curated variant was almost always in our record with the right individuals and zygosity,

549
00:51:04,150 --> 00:51:10,900
and the per-patient detail was richer than OMIM's summary; where it was not, the cause was access or search,

550
00:51:10,900 --> 00:51:11,650
not reading.

551
00:51:11,650 --> 00:51:17,020
A correction: the OMIM check caught a candidate that was a transcript-isoform artefact,

552
00:51:17,020 --> 00:51:22,637
which the ClinVar check could not see because the wrong allele was absent from ClinVar too;

553
00:51:22,637 --> 00:51:24,550
the fix is now in the pipeline.

554
00:51:24,550 --> 00:51:31,624
And a caution: OMIM itself carries legacy numbering and two allelic variants linked to the wrong ClinVar records,

555
00:51:31,624 --> 00:51:35,380
so any concordance figure against OMIM should footnote them,

556
00:51:35,380 --> 00:51:40,513
and a holdout curated by people deserves the same provenance checks as our output.

557
00:51:41,784 --> 00:51:47,593
The report lists nineteen gaps, each with what it blocks, who acts, and a rough cost.

558
00:51:47,593 --> 00:51:50,135
The first six need people, not models.

559
00:51:50,135 --> 00:51:55,959
The primary audit is unrated: two panel members rate 250 claims, roughly ten to fifteen hours each,

560
00:51:55,959 --> 00:52:02,842
and a third breaks ties; it unlocks the only confirmatory outcome and the gatekeeper's accuracy against human labels.

561
00:52:02,842 --> 00:52:09,378
OMIM's phenotype half is unassessed: freezing the six clinical synopsis pages and one harness run would close it.

562
00:52:09,378 --> 00:52:13,009
The 214 candidates are unadjudicated: ten to fifteen minutes each.

563
00:52:13,009 --> 00:52:16,639
The fifty ClinVar misses await triage: two to four hours.

564
00:52:16,639 --> 00:52:19,544
The blinded open-weight audit needs the same raters.

565
00:52:19,544 --> 00:52:24,264
And the bias arm needs four comparator sheets, one to two hours each.

566
00:52:25,514 --> 00:52:30,564
The remaining gaps are about access, inputs, notation and bookkeeping.

567
00:52:30,564 --> 00:52:35,490
Open access only leaves fourteen to fifty-three percent of each corpus readable,

568
00:52:35,490 --> 00:52:38,630
so completeness and the gap count are lower bounds;

569
00:52:38,630 --> 00:52:45,526
adding institutional access would be a deviation and a re-run of a few hundred papers at about two dollars each.

570
00:52:45,526 --> 00:52:51,583
Three extraction gaps were frozen on purpose and should wait for the audit's number: no hedge rule,

571
00:52:51,583 --> 00:52:57,701
relatives' variant fields from the proband's sentence, and cohort statements applied to individuals.

572
00:52:57,701 --> 00:53:00,268
About ten papers had conversion problems,

573
00:53:00,268 --> 00:53:06,526
and notation issues like legacy numbering and variants stated only in prose block tier-one matching.

574
00:53:06,526 --> 00:53:15,235
Thirty-eight of the eighty-four papers OMIM cites for its variants were never returned by our searches because they use pre-gene-era disease names,

575
00:53:15,235 --> 00:53:16,301
a search-term gap.

576
00:53:16,301 --> 00:53:24,018
And several bookkeeping items wait on the principal investigator: the pre-registration tags and OSF registration,

577
00:53:24,018 --> 00:53:29,139
the budget reconciliation, and the drafted messages to Monarch and ClinVar.

578
00:53:30,408 --> 00:53:38,784
The recommended order puts the no-cost human steps first and holds any change to the frozen pipeline until the audit gives a measured failure rate.

579
00:53:38,784 --> 00:53:45,150
Step one: settle the panel and share the audit pages, drawing the open-weight audit sample at the same time.

580
00:53:45,150 --> 00:53:51,792
Step two: freeze the OMIM synopsis pages or submit the API request, push the pre-registration tags, register on OSF,

581
00:53:51,792 --> 00:53:54,197
send the drafts; no cost, long lead times.

582
00:53:54,197 --> 00:53:58,632
Step three: triage the fifty ClinVar misses and the LAD-I candidates first,

583
00:53:58,632 --> 00:54:05,254
because they tell us whether low recall and the biggest gap list are real before the panel spends hours on them.

584
00:54:05,254 --> 00:54:11,377
Step four: the small human tasks that each unblock a registered figure, the comparator sheets, the checklist maps,

585
00:54:11,377 --> 00:54:12,290
the merge groups.

586
00:54:12,290 --> 00:54:16,745
Only after the audit reads: decide on version seven and a re-run, the input repairs,

587
00:54:16,745 --> 00:54:19,661
the notation repairs and the historical-name re-search.

588
00:54:19,661 --> 00:54:23,347
Then the panel adjudicates the remaining candidates, and submissions follow sign-off.

589
00:54:23,347 --> 00:54:30,048
Steps one to four cost no model spend and about sixty to one hundred and ten panel hours in all.

590
00:54:31,298 --> 00:54:34,873
The limitations, stated as the report states them.

591
00:54:34,873 --> 00:54:42,485
There is no human-rated accuracy for the frozen pipeline; every support figure is a model's reading of a model's output.

592
00:54:42,485 --> 00:54:48,985
The development disease is in every table, flagged, but its rules were tuned on its own papers.

593
00:54:48,985 --> 00:54:57,698
OMIM is a sample and only its variant half is in; the patient-level comparison was judged by Claude, not a panel member.

594
00:54:57,698 --> 00:55:04,235
Open access only: the corpora hold fourteen to fifty-three percent of the eligible literature.

595
00:55:04,235 --> 00:55:09,185
Cost figures are session estimates with frozen readings and overlapping stages.

596
00:55:09,185 --> 00:55:15,085
Helper-reported problems are model output, recorded as such and not verified by a person.

597
00:55:15,085 --> 00:55:21,473
And one more, which the report states about itself: the author of the report is the pipeline's author.

598
00:55:21,473 --> 00:55:28,310
Claude Code built the pipeline, ran it, and generated the report and this talk from its outputs.

599
00:55:28,310 --> 00:55:34,960
The numbers are reproducible from the committed files; the framing is not independent of the system it describes.

600
00:55:34,960 --> 00:55:37,948
That is one more reason the human audit decides.

601
00:55:39,266 --> 00:55:40,541
Which brings us to you.

602
00:55:40,541 --> 00:55:45,291
Four roles, each unlocking a registered result or a deliverable.

603
00:55:45,291 --> 00:55:51,659
Audit raters: rate 250 extracted values against their quoted evidence on a blinded web page,

604
00:55:51,659 --> 00:55:59,966
two raters and a tie-breaker, ten to fifteen hours each; this is the primary outcome and the first true accuracy figure.

605
00:55:59,966 --> 00:56:00,840
Adjudicators:

606
00:56:00,840 --> 00:56:08,102
review the 214 candidate variants from their dossiers and decide whether the evidence supports a submission,

607
00:56:08,102 --> 00:56:13,078
ten to fifteen minutes per candidate; this is the gap-closure deliverable.

608
00:56:13,078 --> 00:56:14,102
Cohort readers:

609
00:56:14,102 --> 00:56:22,021
fill a comparator sheet of clinical-feature frequencies from a natural-history paper without looking at our records,

610
00:56:22,021 --> 00:56:25,366
one to two hours per sheet; this is the bias arm.

611
00:56:25,366 --> 00:56:31,016
And methods or domain reviewers: read the paper or the report and tell us what is wrong,

612
00:56:31,016 --> 00:56:34,291
missing or unclear from your field's point of view.

613
00:56:34,291 --> 00:56:38,291
Everything is published literature; there is no patient data.

614
00:56:38,291 --> 00:56:43,478
The dashboard has a short form where you can tell us who you are and how you would like to contribute.

615
00:56:44,786 --> 00:56:45,973
Thank you for listening.

616
00:56:45,973 --> 00:56:52,287
Everything in this talk, the short paper, the full report and its supplement, this deck with its notes,

617
00:56:52,287 --> 00:56:55,536
the candidate list and the form, is on the dashboard.

618
00:56:55,536 --> 00:57:00,175
Every number traces to a file in the project repository at a stated commit,

619
00:57:00,175 --> 00:57:06,174
and the report says plainly which questions are still open: the human audit, the access decision,

620
00:57:06,174 --> 00:57:08,586
and the adjudication of the candidates.

621
00:57:08,586 --> 00:57:10,186
We look forward to working with you.
